{% doc %}
  Renders add-on suggestions inside the cart.

  This is where the business actually makes money. The daily deal is priced at
  or near cost to bring people in, so the margin has to come from what else
  ends up in the basket — and the cart is the highest-intent moment there is.
  Someone here has already decided to buy.

  WHY SAME-VENDOR FIRST. Every Shopify Collective supplier fulfils its own
  items, so a cart mixing vendors arrives as separate parcels on separate days.
  Something from a supplier ALREADY in the cart is the one suggestion that adds
  no extra shipment. That is a genuine reason to say yes rather than a nudge,
  and it turns the multi-parcel problem into an argument for adding more. It
  also gives "similar" for free: a pottery studio's other work resembles the
  pottery already being bought.

  Falls back to a curated collection when no vendor collection resolves, so the
  cart is never left with nothing to suggest.

  Vendor collections are created by scripts/vendors.py and handled
  `vendor-<handleized name>`. The `handleize` filter here has to agree with the
  slug that script writes, or the lookup silently returns nothing.

  @param {string} [fallback] - Handle of a curated collection to use when no
    vendor collection resolves. Put higher-margin items in it.
  @param {number} [limit] - How many suggestions to show. Default 3.
  @param {string} [heading] - Section heading.
  @param {string} [context] - 'drawer' or 'page', used for layout only.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign max_items = limit | default: 3 | plus: 0
  assign layout = context | default: 'drawer'

  comment
    Ids already in the cart, wrapped in commas so a substring cannot match
    (id 12 must not match 123).
  endcomment
  assign in_cart = ','
  for line in cart.items
    assign in_cart = in_cart | append: line.product.id | append: ','
  endfor

  comment
    Vendors present in the cart, in cart order. Do NOT reverse this hoping to
    get "most recently added first" — cart.items is not ordered by when things
    were added, so reversing just picks a different arbitrary vendor to favour.
    Fair representation is handled below instead.
  endcomment
  assign vendor_handles = ''
  for line in cart.items
    assign vendor_handle = line.product.vendor | handleize
    if vendor_handle != blank
      assign probe = '|' | append: vendor_handle | append: '|'
      unless vendor_handles contains probe
        assign vendor_handles = vendor_handles | append: '|' | append: vendor_handle | append: '|'
      endunless
    endif
  endfor
  assign vendor_list = vendor_handles | split: '||'
-%}

{%- capture candidate_ids -%}
  {%- for vendor_handle in vendor_list -%}
    {%- assign clean = vendor_handle | remove: '|' -%}
    {%- if clean == blank -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Build the handle first. A filter cannot run inside a bracket lookup —
      `collections['vendor-' | append: x]` parses as far as the pipe and then
      throws at RENDER time, which theme check does not catch.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign vendor_key = 'vendor-' | append: clean -%}
    {%- assign vendor_collection = collections[vendor_key] -%}
    {%- comment -%}
                  Fifty, not twelve. Vendor collections sort by best-selling, and
                  the tier filters only ever see what this loop yields: measured
                  on Horizon Hat Co., every one of the first thirteen is $27.99
                  and the first item that clears the shipping gap sits at
                  fourteen. Twelve made the clearing tier silently unreachable.
                  Fifty is Shopify's ceiling for an unpaginated collection loop.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- for candidate in vendor_collection.products limit: 50 -%}
      {%- assign probe = ',' | append: candidate.id | append: ',' -%}
      {%- unless in_cart contains probe or candidate.available == false -%}
        {{ candidate.id }},
      {%- endunless -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  assign candidate_list = candidate_ids | split: ','
  assign found = 0
  for id in candidate_list
    assign trimmed = id | strip
    if trimmed != blank
      assign found = found | plus: 1
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    Nothing from the cart's own vendors — a single-item cart from a supplier we
    only carry one product from. Fall back to the curated list rather than
    showing an empty block.
  endcomment
  assign use_fallback = false
  if found == 0 and fallback != blank
    assign use_fallback = true
    assign fallback_collection = collections[fallback]
  endif
-%}

{%- if found > 0 or use_fallback -%}
  <div class="aj-cart-upsell aj-cart-upsell--{{ layout }}">
    <p class="aj-cart-upsell__heading">
      {{ heading | default: 'Add to your order' }}
    </p>

    {%- if found > 0 -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Only claim combined shipping when the suggestion genuinely comes from a
        vendor already in the cart. The fallback list has no such guarantee.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="aj-cart-upsell__note">Ships with what's already in your cart.</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    <ul class="aj-cart-upsell__list" role="list">
      {%- assign shown = 0 -%}

      {%- if use_fallback -%}
        {%- for item in fallback_collection.products limit: 12 -%}
          {%- assign probe = ',' | append: item.id | append: ',' -%}
          {%- if in_cart contains probe or item.available == false -%}
            {%- continue -%}
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if shown >= max_items -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
          {%- assign shown = shown | plus: 1 -%}
          {% render 'aj-cart-upsell-item', item: item %}
        {%- endfor -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Walk the vendor collections directly rather than collecting ids and
          resolving them back to products: `all_products` is keyed by handle,
          so an id round-trip would mean re-walking these same collections
          anyway. Dedupe as we go — a product can sit in two vendor
          collections only if a vendor name changed, but the guard is cheap.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Two passes so a cart holding several suppliers sees several
          suppliers. The first gives every vendor one slot; the second fills
          whatever is left over. Without this the first vendor iterated takes
          every slot, and a two-vendor cart gets three suggestions from one of
          them — which is both less useful and looks broken.

          `cap` is 1 on the first pass and the full limit on the second.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign seen = ',' -%}
        {%- assign passes = '1,99' | split: ',' -%}

        {%- for cap_raw in passes -%}
          {%- assign cap = cap_raw | plus: 0 -%}
          {%- for vendor_handle in vendor_list -%}
            {%- if shown >= max_items -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
            {%- assign clean = vendor_handle | remove: '|' -%}
            {%- if clean == blank -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
            {%- assign vendor_key = 'vendor-' | append: clean -%}
            {%- assign vendor_collection = collections[vendor_key] -%}
            {%- assign from_vendor = 0 -%}
            {%- assign badged = false -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              How far this vendor is from free shipping, in cents.

              The progress line directly above this block may be saying "add
              $27.99 more from X for free shipping". If the suggestions under it
              are a $4 patch and a $6 sticker, the two blocks argue with each
              other — one sets a target and the other ignores it. So anything
              that could plausibly close the gap is offered first.

              Zero when this vendor has no reachable threshold, which mutes the
              whole preference and leaves the original behaviour intact.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- liquid
              assign gap_cents = 0
              assign vs = nil
              for entry in shop.metaobjects.vendor_shipping.values
                # Filters cannot run inside an `if` condition — assign first.
                assign entry_handle = entry.vendor.value | handleize
                if entry_handle == clean
                  assign vs = entry
                  break
                endif
              endfor
              if vs and vs.shape.value == 'threshold' and vs.reachable.value
                assign vendor_cents = 0
                for line in cart.items
                  assign line_handle = line.product.vendor | handleize
                  if line_handle == clean
                    assign vendor_cents = vendor_cents | plus: line.final_line_price
                  endif
                endfor
                assign threshold_cents = vs.free_threshold.value | times: 100 | round
                if threshold_cents > vendor_cents
                  assign gap_cents = threshold_cents | minus: vendor_cents
                endif
              endif
              # Two bars. `gap_clear` actually unlocks free shipping; `gap_floor`
              # only makes visible progress toward it.
              #
              # gap_clear carries a dollar of headroom because the threshold is
              # bisected to about a dollar, not to the cent. Without it, an item
              # priced at exactly the gap can land two cents short — measured:
              # a $27.99 hat against a $27.99 gap totals $55.98 on a $56
              # threshold, and the shopper adds it and gets nothing.
              assign gap_clear = gap_cents | plus: 100
              assign gap_floor = gap_cents | times: 60 | divided_by: 100
            -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              Tier 2 clears the threshold outright, tier 1 only makes progress
              toward it, tier 0 is anything. Nested inside the vendor loop rather
              than outside it so the two-pass vendor fairness above is untouched —
              this changes WHICH item a supplier contributes, never how many.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign tiers = '2,1,0' | split: ',' -%}
            {%- for tier_raw in tiers -%}
              {%- assign tier = tier_raw | plus: 0 -%}
              {%- if tier > 0 and gap_cents == 0 -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                  Fifty, not twelve. Vendor collections sort by best-selling, and
                  the tier filters only ever see what this loop yields: measured
                  on Horizon Hat Co., every one of the first thirteen is $27.99
                  and the first item that clears the shipping gap sits at
                  fourteen. Twelve made the clearing tier silently unreachable.
                  Fifty is Shopify's ceiling for an unpaginated collection loop.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- for candidate in vendor_collection.products limit: 50 -%}
                {%- if shown >= max_items or from_vendor >= cap -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
                {%- assign probe = ',' | append: candidate.id | append: ',' -%}
                {%- if in_cart contains probe or seen contains probe -%}
                  {%- continue -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- if candidate.available == false -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
                {%- if tier == 2 and candidate.price < gap_clear -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
                {%- if tier == 1 and candidate.price < gap_floor -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
                {%- assign seen = seen | append: candidate.id | append: ',' -%}
                {%- assign shown = shown | plus: 1 -%}
                {%- assign from_vendor = from_vendor | plus: 1 -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  Tier 2 means this item clears the threshold outright. That was
                  already true and completely invisible — the row that unlocks
                  free shipping rendered identically to one that does not, so all
                  the tiering above did was change the order of three items
                  nobody could tell apart. Saying so is the entire value of it.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  ONE BADGE, NOT THREE. Every tier-2 item clears the threshold,
                  so the first version marked all three suggestions and the
                  cart rendered a wall of identical yellow labels. A badge on
                  everything is a badge on nothing -- it stops reading as "this
                  one solves your problem" and starts reading as decoration.
                  The list is already ordered cheapest-first within the tier, so
                  the first one marked is also the least someone has to spend.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- assign clears = false -%}
                {%- if tier == 2 and gap_cents > 0 and badged != true -%}
                  {%- assign clears = true -%}
                  {%- assign badged = true -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                {% render 'aj-cart-upsell-item', item: candidate, clears_gap: clears %}
              {%- endfor -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    </ul>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
